{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The code is meant to be thrown away, and the only goal is to learn whether an idea works, so speed matters more than anything. Accepting AI output without reading it is fine as long as nobody quietly turns the prototype into the real product."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "A demo is judged by how it behaves in front of an audience, not by its internals, so judging it only by whether it works is reasonable. The exception is anything that touches real credentials, real data, or a public deployment: skim those parts for secrets and obvious security holes, and make sure the demo doesn't promise anything the code doesn't actually do."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, security exposure, and code that later work will be built on, so 'it seems to work' isn't enough anymore. Put automated tests on the core flows and review the risky areas (auth, payments, data handling, data model and schema decisions) closely, while moving faster on low-risk UI and glue code."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "AI can write most of the code, but a person who owns the change must understand it well enough to maintain and debug it, and nothing merges without normal code review and CI tests. Scale the review to the risk: security-sensitive code, data migrations, concurrency, and public APIs get line-by-line review (level 2), while routine, well-tested changes can be reviewed selectively."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, every line has to be understood, traceable, and defensible to auditors, so AI can help write code but a qualified person must review each change line by line on top of the usual verification process. For the most safety-critical parts, or where regulations or certification rules (e.g., DO-178C, IEC 62304, ISO 26262) limit tool-generated code, drop to level 1 or 0."
    }
  },
  "summary": "How much to trust AI-written code without reading it should depend on how costly a hidden defect would be and how long the code will live. Vibe coding freely (level 4) is fine for throwaway prototypes and demos; once real users depend on the software, switch to AI-written code backed by tests and review focused on risk (level 3); critical systems need line-by-line human review (level 2) or stricter. Two rules apply at every stage: the person who merges code is accountable for it no matter who or what wrote it, and code from an earlier stage has to be brought up to the next stage's standard before it's promoted, not carried forward as is."
}

